title start
kind: research
tags: business-plan research consistency-and-recognition-evidence
Does a consistent look make a brand easier to recognise
title end

section start
title start
Abstract
title end
paragraph start
Question: does a consistent look make a brand easier to recognise. Method: a review of colour in brand communication and a doctoral study summary. Result: consistent colour use is reported to speed up and sharpen recognition, and familiarity predicts early attention. Limit: both sources are secondary and none was about social video.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Introduction
title end
paragraph start
The brand runs many accounts, so this page asks whether one shared look is worth enforcing.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Methods
title end
paragraph start
Read two secondary sources on recognition.
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Results
title end
paragraph start
A review of colour in brand communication reports that consistent colour use increases the speed and accuracy of brand recognition.
paragraph end
paragraph start
A 2025 doctoral study found that familiar brands are prioritised early by visual attention and chosen more (Hagen-Aga, 2025).
paragraph end
section end

section start
title start
Discussion
title end
paragraph start
This supports one fixed palette, one overlay set and one logo position across all accounts, even when the topics differ.
paragraph end
paragraph start
todo: check the recognition claim in a primary source
Secondary only.
paragraph end
